{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conductive heat flux direction conductive heat flux direction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","fileName":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 00:55\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Conduction Convection and Radiation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","COMSOL Point Line and Surface Evaluations","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conductive heat flux direction conductive heat flux direction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","fileName":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction","heading":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The temperature gradient points toward the direction of greatest temperature increase, while conductive heat flux points oppositely. In three-dimensional Cartesian space, separate temperature derivatives in $x$, $y$, and $z$ define the components of this vector relationship.\n\nTotal conductive power through a surface uses the component normal to that surface. Thus an $x$-directed heat flux crosses a plane parallel to $y$ and $z$, a geometric relationship that must be preserved in boundary selections and [[COMSOL Point Line and Surface Evaluations|surface evaluations]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","COMSOL Point Line and Surface Evaluations","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conductive heat flux direction conductive heat flux direction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","fileName":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conductive Heat Flux Direction","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","COMSOL Point Line and Surface Evaluations","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}